    "rawText": "John Smith\n123 -456 - 7890 | # j ohn . smith@example.com | ï l in ked in . com/in/j ohn -smith | * Aust in , TX\nEDUCATION\nUniversity of Texas at Austin Austin, TX\nBS, Computer Science (Dean's List) Aug. 2022 – May 2026\nEXPERIENCE\nSearch Infrastructure Intern Austin, TX\nLatticework Systems May 2025 – Aug 2025\n• Query latency in the ranking service was dominated by cold cache misses; adding a tiered LRU cache cut p99 latency\nfrom 480ms to 90ms\n• Built a Go-based indexing pipeline that reprocesses 40M documents nightly, replacing a 14-hour full rebuild with a\n2-hour incremental one\n• Wrote a relevance-scoring harness that A/B tests ranking changes against 25 query sets before rollout\n• Traced a tokenizer bug that was silently dropping non-ASCII terms, recovering recall on roughly 6% of queries\n• Added query-shape validation to the ingestion API so malformed filters get rejected before hitting the index\nMobile App Lead Austin, TX\nLonghorn Founders Guild Sep. 2024 – May 2025\n• Rebuilt the club's iOS/Android app in React Native , cutting cold-start time from 4.2s to 1 .1 s\n• Added offline sync with conflict resolution, cutting failed submissions over flaky campus wifi by roughly 90%\n• Shipped push notifications for event reminders, lifting RSVP show-up rate from 61% to 84%\n• Led a team of 4 volunteer developers to ship a member directory used by 2,100+ students, now averaging 4.7 stars\nacross app stores\nSecurity Contributor Austin, TX\nLonghorn Security Collective May 2024 – Aug 2024\n• Ran a dependency audit across the club's stack, finding and patching 9 vulnerable packages before a public writeup\ncould flag them\n• Added rate limiting and CSRF protection to the member portal, closing 2 exploitable auth-bypass paths\n• Wrote a secrets-scanning pre-commit hook that's blocked 30+ accidental credential leaks since rollout\nPROJECTS\nTrail Route Planner - React, TypeScript, Node.js, PostgreSQL Feb. 2026 – present\n• Built a closure-aware routing engine on top of OpenStreetMap data for 500+ hikers\n• A* search with a custom elevation-gain heuristic replaced brute-force pathfinding, cutting route computation from 12s\nto under 1 s\n• Users can report closures live; affected edges re-weight instantly instead of triggering a full graph rebuild\nCampus Event Finder - React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, Firebase Sep. 2024 – Dec. 2024\n• Consolidated 9 separate club calendars into one searchable feed used by 1 ,100+ students\n• Added tag-based filtering backed by a denormalized Firestore index for instant search\n• Push notifications for saved searches lifted week-over-week return visits roughly 3x\nChess Endgame Solver - C++ Jan. 2023 – Feb. 2023\n• Bitboard-based minimax with alpha-beta pruning and a transposition table solves 5-piece endgames in under 2\nseconds\n• Took 1 st of 60 submissions in a department programming contest judged on correctness and speed\nSKILLS\nLanguages : JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Java, C/C++, SQL\nFrameworks & Tools : React, Node.js, Django, Docker, Git, Linux, AWS, CI/CD",
    "redactedText": "**** *****\n*** **** * **** | # * *** * ***************** * * * ** *** ** * ******** *** ****** | * **** ** * **\nEDUCATION\nUniversity of Texas at Austin Austin, TX\nBS, Computer Science (Dean's List) Aug. 2022 – May 2026\nEXPERIENCE\nSearch Infrastructure Intern Austin, TX\nLatticework Systems May 2025 – Aug 2025\n• Query latency in the ranking service was dominated by cold cache misses; adding a tiered LRU cache cut p99 latency\nfrom 480ms to 90ms\n• Built a Go-based indexing pipeline that reprocesses 40M documents nightly, replacing a 14-hour full rebuild with a\n2-hour incremental one\n• Wrote a relevance-scoring harness that A/B tests ranking changes against 25 query sets before rollout\n• Traced a tokenizer bug that was silently dropping non-ASCII terms, recovering recall on roughly 6% of queries\n• Added query-shape validation to the ingestion API so malformed filters get rejected before hitting the index\nMobile App Lead Austin, TX\nLonghorn Founders Guild Sep. 2024 – May 2025\n• Rebuilt the club's iOS/Android app in React Native , cutting cold-start time from 4.2s to 1 .1 s\n• Added offline sync with conflict resolution, cutting failed submissions over flaky campus wifi by roughly 90%\n• Shipped push notifications for event reminders, lifting RSVP show-up rate from 61% to 84%\n• Led a team of 4 volunteer developers to ship a member directory used by 2,100+ students, now averaging 4.7 stars\nacross app stores\nSecurity Contributor Austin, TX\nLonghorn Security Collective May 2024 – Aug 2024\n• Ran a dependency audit across the club's stack, finding and patching 9 vulnerable packages before a public writeup\ncould flag them\n• Added rate limiting and CSRF protection to the member portal, closing 2 exploitable auth-bypass paths\n• Wrote a secrets-scanning pre-commit hook that's blocked 30+ accidental credential leaks since rollout\nPROJECTS\nTrail Route Planner - React, TypeScript, Node.js, PostgreSQL Feb. 2026 – present\n• Built a closure-aware routing engine on top of OpenStreetMap data for 500+ hikers\n• A* search with a custom elevation-gain heuristic replaced brute-force pathfinding, cutting route computation from 12s\nto under 1 s\n• Users can report closures live; affected edges re-weight instantly instead of triggering a full graph rebuild\nCampus Event Finder - React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, Firebase Sep. 2024 – Dec. 2024\n• Consolidated 9 separate club calendars into one searchable feed used by 1 ,100+ students\n• Added tag-based filtering backed by a denormalized Firestore index for instant search\n• Push notifications for saved searches lifted week-over-week return visits roughly 3x\nChess Endgame Solver - C++ Jan. 2023 – Feb. 2023\n• Bitboard-based minimax with alpha-beta pruning and a transposition table solves 5-piece endgames in under 2\nseconds\n• Took 1 st of 60 submissions in a department programming contest judged on correctness and speed\nSKILLS\nLanguages : JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Java, C/C++, SQL\nFrameworks & Tools : React, Node.js, Django, Docker, Git, Linux, AWS, CI/CD",
